+## Professional practice: outcomes, audit, patient safety, statistics, genomics and sustainability
+Added 21 November 2026. The 2026 curriculum (above) adds genomics, clinical informatics and sustainability as emerging areas, and CiP 7 asks trainees to assess personal and unit outcomes; the FRCS Atlas chapter 54 covers this ground but the course did not. Facts were checked on 21 November 2026 against the named sources, mostly through search extracts of the primary pages; the full PDFs were not re-read.
+
+### National audit and outcome monitoring (CiP 7)
+- Cardiac surgery: the **National Adult Cardiac Surgery Audit (NACSA)**, part of NICOR, collects every adult cardiac operation and publishes risk-adjusted outcomes by unit and surgeon. Thoracic surgery: the SCTS thoracic audit and the National Lung Cancer Audit (NLCA). Risk adjustment compares observed with expected deaths from a risk model (for example EuroSCORE II).
+- **Funnel plots**: each unit or surgeon is plotted against volume, with control limits that narrow as volume rises. HQIP outlier guidance for national audits (updated November 2025): **alert** = more than **2 SD** from expected (about the 95% limit); **alarm** = more than **3 SD** (the **99.8%** limit, as shown on NACSA funnel plots). An alarm triggers data validation, notification of the trust and, if confirmed, review and publication.
+- **VLAD** (variable life-adjusted display): running total of expected minus observed deaths, so the line rises when outcomes are better than predicted. **CUSUM**: cumulative sum that signals when it crosses a preset boundary; both give real-time monitoring between annual reports.
+
+### Patient safety
+- **WHO Surgical Safety Checklist** (Haynes et al., N Engl J Med 2009;360:491; 7,688 patients in 8 hospitals): deaths **1.5% to 0.8%**, inpatient complications **11.0% to 7.0%**. Checked: PubMed abstract.
+- **Never Events** (NHS England framework, revised 2018, list updated February 2021): wholly preventable serious incidents such as wrong-site surgery, wrong implant or prosthesis, retained foreign object after a procedure and misplaced nasogastric tube. A consultation opened in February 2024; findings published September 2025 (two-thirds of respondents called the framework unfit for purpose). It **remains in force** until a replacement is issued. Checked: NHS England publication page, via search extract.
+- **PSIRF** (Patient Safety Incident Response Framework, NHS England, **August 2022**) replaced the 2015 Serious Incident Framework: proportionate, systems-based learning responses (patient safety incident investigation, after-action review, swarm huddles) and compassionate engagement with patients, families and staff, in place of routine root-cause analysis reports. Checked: NHS England, via search extract.
+- **Statutory Duty of Candour** (Regulation 20, Health and Social Care Act 2008 (Regulated Activities) Regulations 2014; applies to the organisation). A notifiable safety incident is unintended or unexpected, occurs in a regulated activity, and in a health professional's reasonable opinion has caused or could cause **death, severe harm, moderate harm or prolonged psychological harm** (prolonged = at least 28 continuous days). Steps: tell the person **in person** as soon as reasonably practicable, give an account and explain further enquiries, offer support, **apologise**, then follow up with a **written notification** and keep records. CQC: an apology is not an admission of liability (see also Compensation Act 2006 s2). Individual clinicians have a separate professional duty of candour (GMC and NMC guidance). Checked: CQC Regulation 20 guidance and legislation.gov.uk, via search extract.
+
+### Statistics for the viva
+- **ARR** = control event rate minus treatment event rate; **RRR** = ARR divided by control rate; **NNT** = 1/ARR. Worked from Haynes: ARR for death 1.5 − 0.8 = 0.7 percentage points, RRR about 47%, NNT about 143 operations per death avoided.
+- **Hazard ratio**: relative instantaneous event rate over follow-up; report with its confidence interval (see the trial appraisal steps above for 90% against 95% intervals).
+- **Sensitivity and specificity**: **SnNout** (a highly sensitive test, if negative, rules the condition out) and **SpPin** (a highly specific test, if positive, rules it in). Predictive values change with prevalence; sensitivity and specificity do not.
+- **Non-inferiority trials**: a prespecified margin and a one-sided test. Intention-to-treat analysis is not conservative here, because crossover and non-adherence pull the arms together and favour a non-inferiority conclusion; check that the **per-protocol** analysis agrees. Standard methodology (CONSORT extension for non-inferiority trials).
+
+### Genomics
+- Delivered through the **NHS Genomic Medicine Service** and the National Genomic Test Directory, with documented consent covering incidental findings and implications for relatives. Variants are classified pathogenic, likely pathogenic, **uncertain significance (VUS)**, likely benign or benign (ACMG/AMP); a VUS should not by itself change surgical thresholds or trigger cascade testing. The clinical uses are elsewhere in the course: heritable thoracic aortic disease genes and gene-specific thresholds (Cardiac 6), EGFR, ALK and PD-L1 testing in lung cancer (Thoracic 4), and 22q11 deletion and other syndromes (Congenital). Checked: standard practice; no single primary source.
+
+### Sustainability
+- **Desflurane**: NHS England announced in January 2023 that it would stop using it; from **1 April 2024** use is restricted to exceptional cases. NHS Scotland stopped buying it in March 2023. Prefer intravenous anaesthesia or low-flow sevoflurane; reduce nitrous oxide use and leakage. Checked: NHS England guidance (March 2024), via search extract; the Scotland date from a secondary source.
+- The NHS net zero targets (*Delivering a Net Zero National Health Service*, 2020): **2040** for the emissions the NHS controls directly and **2045** for those it can influence. In theatre: reusable rather than single-use equipment where safe, rationalised custom packs, waste segregation, switching off equipment and ventilation when theatres are empty. The intercollegiate Green Theatre Checklist (2022) collects these measures. Checked: targets and checklist from the published documents as recalled; not re-read.
