+## Shunt antiplatelet therapy and cavopulmonary criteria: gaps filled from the Atlas audit
+Added 21 November 2026 after auditing all 54 FRCS Atlas chapters against this site: only points the site lacked. Each line says what it was checked against.
+
+### After a modified Blalock-Taussig-Thomas shunt
+- Heparin infusion in the early postoperative period, then **low-dose aspirin** for as long as the shunt is in place (dose by unit protocol). Shunt thrombosis is the main early killer; the rescue sequence is above.
+- **CLARINET** (Wessel et al., N Engl J Med 2013;368:2377): adding clopidogrel to standard care (mostly aspirin) in infants with systemic-to-pulmonary shunts did **not** reduce death or shunt thrombosis. Checked: trial result as recalled; not re-read.
+
+### Haemodynamic criteria before Glenn and Fontan
+- Both depend on passive pulmonary flow, so pulmonary resistance must be low: for Fontan, mean PA pressure about **15 mmHg or less**, transpulmonary gradient about 6 to 8 mmHg or less and **PVR under about 2 to 3 Wood units·m²**, with good ventricular function, a competent AV valve and unobstructed pulmonary arteries. Choussat's original 1977 criteria included mean PA pressure 15 mmHg or less and PVR under 4 Wood units·m². This tab's Fontan criteria say only "low pulmonary resistance". Checked: textbook thresholds as recalled; units vary between sources.
+
+### Signs that a Fontan is failing
+- Rising systemic venous (Fontan) pressure, commonly quoted as over about 15 to 18 mmHg; protein-losing enteropathy (low albumin; **24-hour stool alpha-1 antitrypsin clearance**, as in the 2025 ACC/AHA guideline); plastic bronchitis; Fontan-associated liver disease; atrial arrhythmia; cyanosis from venovenous collaterals; ventricular or AV-valve failure. Referral to a transplant team is in the ACHD tab.
